{
  "markdown_report": "The first experiment should test **stochastic old-prefix removal during GRU4Rec training**, with controls separating context removal from discarded supervision. This remains a hypothesis about harmful persistence, not an established remedy.\n\n**Starting insights.** (1) Feedback favors recent coherent activity, motivating a temporal-context intervention; it does not establish that older evidence causes errors. (2) Existing margins and embedding geometry do not measure temporal dependence; paired ranking interventions can supplement them. (3) Available metadata cannot establish compatibility, unmet need, or mood; report observable evidence and uncertainty.\n\n**Related-work insights.** (1) TRACE motivates counterfactual ranking comparisons [1], but these measure model dependence rather than user intent. (2) Temporal-aware contrastive work warns that augmentations disrupt sequence semantics [2]; suffix removal preserves retained chronology but still changes context. (3) Sampled metrics can reverse comparisons [3], so diagnostic candidates must remain paired. (4) Recurrent deletion augmentation and recency sampling provide prior art [4,5]; neither establishes an isolated benefit from this intervention.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Retain three axes: temporal dependence, candidate discrimination, and evidence validity. Cross temporal interventions with supervision controls: cropped-context/retained-target training versus full-context/identical-target training isolates access to old context; full-context/all-target training measures the separate supervision effect. Candidate controls and evidence limits apply throughout.\n\n**Candidate ideas and assessments.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Controlled old-prefix dropout: 3/6/4.** Originality: focused context–supervision separation, but truncation is established. Potential: inexpensive and interpretable, but can erase useful preferences. Difficulty: small augmentation, but exposure matching and padding require care [1,2,4,5].\n- **Lag-stratified intervention probe: 4/6/3.** Originality: paired temporal controls, but established counterfactual principles. Potential: guides revisions, but adds no predictive capacity. Difficulty: reuses inference, but requires candidate and length controls [1].\n- **Within-model hard-negative training: 3/6/4.** Originality: familiar mining. Potential: strengthens discrimination, but unobserved items can be false negatives. Difficulty: existing scorer suffices, but mining costs computation [3].\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Construct aligned next-item pairs first. With probability 0.25, retain a suffix of uniformly sampled length from ceil(n/2) through n; bypass n≤4. Otherwise retain all positions. This conservative setting replaces the original minimum-two rule; neither distribution is literature-validated. Reset hidden states to zero, preserve alignment, and bypass padding recurrence [6]. Use token-mean loss over valid retained targets.\n\nPseudocode: `plan retained positions and negatives; crop aligned tensors; left_pad; reset_state; masked_GRU; sum(mask*loss)/sum(mask)`.\n\nCompare against the corrected cold start and a full-context arm using identical target masks, negatives, denominators, and cumulative target exposures. An optional fixed-truncation control should match aggregate retained lengths. Freeze settings before validation; avoid adaptive searches across length strata. Full-history inference is primary; suffix inference is diagnostic only. Report short-history effects and paired training-prefix deletion responses without claiming user-intent causality.\n\nPreserve seeds, four routes, ≤200 epochs, 200 batches/epoch, final-nonpadding readout, diagnostic interfaces, and predictor/checkpoint consistency. Each trial uses one model/category. Selection remains pinned macro validation HR@10; no improvement is claimed before measurement.\n\n[1] [TRACE](https://github.com/devUuung/TRACE). [2] [Temporal-Aware and Intent Contrastive Learning](https://www.mdpi.com/2073-8994/17/10/1634). [3] [On Sampled Metrics](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/). [4] [Improved Recurrent Neural Networks](https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.08117). [5] [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643). [6] [PyTorch GRU](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.GRU.html).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Test stochastic old-prefix removal in the corrected GRU4Rec, using a matched-target full-context control to distinguish historical-context effects from changes in supervision. Preserve the backbone and deploy exactly one selected checkpoint per category.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback motivates testing whether old context displaces immediate matches, but does not establish that mechanism. The original proposal simultaneously removed context and supervised targets, confounding its interpretation. A matched-target control isolates access to earlier history for retained predictions. Results cannot establish compatibility, functional redundancy, mood, or actual user intent.",
    "implementation_notes": "Use only permitted training histories. Construct input/next-item pairs and baseline negatives before augmentation. For each row, let n be its number of valid input positions. Bypass n<=4; otherwise crop with probability 0.25, retaining k uniformly from ceil(n/2) through n. Slice inputs, positives, negatives, and existing loss masks at identical original positions, then restore left padding. This replaces the earlier uniform 2..n proposal to reduce aggressive short-history corruption; the new settings remain unvalidated hypotheses. Use a separately seeded augmentation generator with seed 42 so augmentation does not perturb baseline negative-sampling randomness. Initialize every independently sampled sequence to zero hidden state; detachment alone is insufficient. Padding must bypass recurrence at every recurrent layer, either through masked GRUCell updates or valid-sequence compaction followed by packing and output remapping. Zero padding embeddings alone are insufficient. Packing directly left-padded arrays by nonzero counts is incorrect. Preserve the corrected baseline's behavior when it already satisfies these requirements. Keep log2feats outputs aligned to original B x L positions. Read the maximum nonpadding input index, never nonzero_count-1; handle empty histories explicitly using the baseline's defined behavior. Compute loss as sum(mask*per_target_loss)/sum(mask), excluding padded inputs and missing targets; skip empty-loss batches without a division by zero. Keep optimizer, baseline loss function, negative sampler, and architecture unchanged in the primary intervention. For the matched full-context control, reuse the same original batches, retained-target plans, negatives, and loss denominators, but preserve all input history and mask only the loss. This matches supervised target positions and counts update by update. Report cumulative valid target exposures and sampled-user frequencies. The corrected full-context/all-target baseline deliberately has more supervision; report that difference rather than calling it exposure matched. A separate exposure-matched all-target comparison requires a prespecified training-only target-selection plan and cannot exceed 200 epochs or 200 batches per epoch. An optional fixed-truncation control should match aggregate retained context lengths and supervised-target budgets; one fixed K need not achieve this across heterogeneous histories. Run experimental arms as separate host trials without reading or reusing other trials' checkpoints; never combine their predictions. Prioritize the intervention and matched-target control within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], artifacts beneath the trial directory, and all four category routes. Each category trains and loads one model/checkpoint. Preserve ModelView, item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Return measured metrics with definitions; unavailable samples must not appear as healthy zero-error measurements. Paired temporal probes use training-only prefixes with targets withheld from inputs, identical candidate sets, equal-count recent/old deletions where possible, and restored padding. Report ranking changes alongside hidden-state changes; deletion also changes context length. Stratify by original history length and observed timestamp gaps, and report full-history versus suffix-inference sensitivity as diagnostics. Freeze augmentation settings before validation and avoid selecting on many strata or diagnostic variants. Retain the host's pinned macro validation HR@10 as the sole selection objective. Full-history inference is the primary deployment setting, and its mismatch with cropped training is an empirical risk. The embedded predictor writer must exactly reproduce the trained architecture, padding, reset, and readout behavior. No separately fitted popularity, transition, or other predictor may be fused with neural scores.",
    "pseudocode": "augmentation_rng = independent_rng(seed=42)\n# Separate host trials; each trains one GRU4Rec per category.\nfor each baseline batch (None, sequence, positive, negative):\n    original_valid = (sequence != 0)\n    target_valid = original_valid & (positive != 0)\n    retain = original_valid.clone()\n    for row in batch:\n        positions = nonpadding_positions(sequence[row])\n        n = len(positions)\n        if n > 4 and augmentation_rng.uniform() < 0.25:\n            k = augmentation_rng.randint_inclusive(ceil(n / 2), n)\n            retain[row] = mask_for(positions[-k:])\n    supervised_mask = target_valid & retain\n    # Replay the same batch/plan/negatives in the matched control trial.\n    if arm == 'cropped_context':\n        x, y, neg, mask = aligned_suffix_slice_and_left_pad(\n            sequence, positive, negative, supervised_mask, retain)\n    elif arm == 'full_context_matched_targets':\n        x, y, neg, mask = sequence, positive, negative, supervised_mask\n    elif arm == 'corrected_all_target_baseline':\n        x, y, neg, mask = sequence, positive, negative, target_valid\n    h0 = zeros_for_every_sample_and_layer()\n    features = padding_bypassing_gru_log2feats(x, h0)\n    per_target_loss = baseline_loss_without_reduction(features, y, neg)\n    if mask.sum() > 0:\n        loss = per_target_loss[mask].sum() / mask.sum()\n        baseline_optimizer_step(loss)\n    record_valid_target_exposures(mask.sum())\n# Primary inference: full permitted history, zero initial state,\n# padding-bypassing recurrence, final nonpadding feature.\n# Host evaluates each trial independently; never fuse checkpoints or scores.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "The specific controlled comparison separates removed historical context from removed supervision in an every-position GRU4Rec objective.",
      "negative": "Recurrent deletion augmentation, suffix truncation, and recency sampling are established; no verified source establishes this exact control as a new contribution."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "A controlled result could guide inexpensive, category-sensitive studies of when recurrent recommendation benefits from shorter context.",
      "negative": "Benefits remain unmeasured, the intervention can discard useful long-term evidence, and validation gains would not establish improved real-user intent matching."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The intervention retains the supplied GRU4Rec architecture and requires only aligned cropping and explicit loss masking.",
      "negative": "Reproducible matched controls, target-exposure accounting, padding bypass, and diagnostic/predictor consistency require more care than the original sampler-only estimate suggested."
    }
  },
